WEBVTT

00:00.775 --> 00:03.100
This is Cortex. It's memory for work.

00:03.150 --> 00:06.575
When I sit down with a new session, it doesn't start the project over.

00:06.625 --> 00:09.325
It starts from where the work actually is.

00:10.192 --> 00:12.567
I tell it about a project in plain lines.

00:12.617 --> 00:14.992
First, what the project is.

00:15.901 --> 00:19.476
Then a checkpoint: a piece of the work with a clear edge.

00:21.901 --> 00:25.276
What I expect to be true when that checkpoint is done.

00:27.901 --> 00:29.314
And where it stands today.

00:29.364 --> 00:32.388
Each of those lines is a run of its own, with its own time.

00:32.438 --> 00:33.826
Nothing here is a document.

00:33.876 --> 00:35.401
It's all state.

00:36.276 --> 00:40.389
As a session works, it reports what it did, as a line, and Cortex reads

00:40.439 --> 00:42.651
it against what it already holds.

00:44.785 --> 00:46.685
Now I say the same thing twice.

00:46.735 --> 00:48.410
It isn't stored twice.

00:48.460 --> 00:51.785
It's the same memory with a new time on it, and that is how it tells time

00:51.835 --> 00:54.010
passing from new information.

00:54.902 --> 00:57.927
When the checkpoint is done, I say so, and the state moves.

00:57.977 --> 00:59.727
The history stays.

01:01.886 --> 01:05.261
The next session opens the project and finds the state, the time, and the

01:05.311 --> 01:07.574
grounding. Nothing to re-read.

01:07.624 --> 01:11.286
Cortex, by James Huson, at exnulla dot com.
